<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sultan is an Arabic title meaning authority or power, used by Muslim rulers. The Seljuk rulers were among the first to use it as a formal title, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. It was held by the rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a>, among others.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan.jpg" alt="Sultan" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Circle of Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EmperorSuleiman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word derives from an Arabic and Semitic root meaning to be hard or strong. The noun sulṭān first designated a kind of moral authority or spiritual power, as opposed to political power, and it is used in this sense several times in the Qur'an. In the early Muslim world ultimate authority was held in theory by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, while local governors bearing the title amir were appointed by him. After the 8th century political fragmentation challenged this arrangement, and in the 9th century some governors became de facto independent and founded dynasties, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a>. Towards the late 10th century the term began to be used for an individual ruler with practically sovereign authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first major figure to clearly grant himself the title was the Ghaznavid ruler Mahmud, who reigned from 998 to 1030. The Great Seljuks adopted it soon afterwards, and their early leader Tughril Bey was the first to use the epithet sultan on his coinage. Their power overshadowed that of the Abbasid caliphs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, which led scholars such as Al-Juwayni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ghazali">Al-Ghazali</a> to argue that legitimate authority derived from the caliph but was delegated to sovereign rulers he recognised.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258, the surviving Abbasid descendants lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> protection, and the Mamluks styled themselves sultans. In the 16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> conquered the Mamluks, and the jurist Ebüssuûd Mehmet Efendi recognised the Ottoman sultan Suleiman as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> as well as sultan. In the 19th century Ottoman authorities emphasised this combination, claiming that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> received the caliphate in Cairo in 1517. Shi'a rulers did not use it as a sovereign title: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> used the Persian title shah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> themselves usually called their ruler padişah, and the formal title combined sultan with khan. By the 16th century the title was carried by men and women of the Ottoman dynasty, and Ottoman princesses and the mother of the reigning sultan bore it after their names. In the Kazakh Khanate a sultan was a prince descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. Sultanates stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to the Malay world. The monarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> changed his title from sultan to king in 1957, and Brunei, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> are the sovereign states that retain the title.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sultan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The shadow caliphate continued in Cairo under Mamluk patronage until 1517, when the Ottoman <b>Sultan</b> Selim I's conquest of Egypt brought even that ceremonial institution to an end.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Acehnese tradition dates the founding of the sultanate to 1496, but its real rise began under <b>Sultan</b> Ali Mughayat Syah, who ruled from about 1514 until 1530.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The capital lay at Dakkar until <b>Sultan</b> Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad moved it to Harar in 1520, part of a gradual reorientation towards the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Saladin's nephew, <b>Sultan</b> al-Kamil, later handed Jerusalem to Emperor Frederick II by treaty in 1229, in return for help against his own Ayyubid relatives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Brunei's own tradition, preserved in the royal genealogy and the epic Syair Awang Semaun, holds that its first ruler, Awang Alak Betatar, accepted Islam and became <b>Sultan</b> Muhammad Shah, an event traditionally placed in the 1360s; the register uses 1368.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 17th century, after the royal family converted to Bani Islam, kings also used the Malay title Paduka Seri <b>Sultan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
